WEBVTT

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:09.970
 And I remember very clearly being up in Yolo County, up outside of Winters, with this amazing man, John Anderson.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:17.440
 And so he started this heroic effort, really, of bringing all the edges of his farm back to life.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:27.270
 And I remember standing on one side of the road, and there were John Anderson's irrigation ditches that completely had grasses and forbs and sedges.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:31.130
 And, you know, they were starting to have some structure on them.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:34.390
 And they supported ducks and all kinds of insects.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:41.570
 They filtered the dirt that was coming off the roads or any kinds of chemicals that were, might go back into the canal.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:46.790
 He was so, so careful about how water came on and off of his property.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:51.890
 And it was basically managed by putting vegetation back on the land.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:57.910
 And you would look across the road and there was the exact opposite, right?

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:06.350
 Just completely bare, irrigation canal, crops, nothing else but maybe, you know, canning tomatoes or something like that.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:17.580
 And you consider that there's 20,000 miles of irrigation canals running through California and we could really have a completely different kind of agriculture.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:31.060
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:32.060
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:38.180
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.480
 These people have shaped the food movement in California.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:52.120
 We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.980
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:01.540
 Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:09.320
 Dan Imhoff is an author, musician, and artisan food producer who has written for 25 years on ecological sustainability.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.400
 His books include Farming with the Wild, Food Fight, and Building with Vision.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:28.530
 Dan is the president and co-founder of Watershed Media, as well as president and co-founder of Wild Farm Alliance, a national organization that works to promote agriculture systems that support and accommodate wild nature.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.190
 He lives on a small homestead farm outside of Healdsburg, California.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.330
 My name is Dan Imhoff.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:42.770
 I am the co-founder and president of Watershed Media here in Healdsburg, California.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:50.430
 And I'm also the co-founder and the president of a more national organization called the Wild Farm Alliance.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:51.970
 It's based in Watsonville.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:52.490
 Wonderful.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.550
 Thanks so much for being with us on Delicious Revolution, Dan.

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:56.550
 No problem.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:01.930
 I was doing some last-minute research this morning, and I came across a TED Talk that you gave.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:06.710
 And there's two little details that I think that maybe I bet we'll get along.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:09.620
 One is that you mentioned that you just love visiting farms.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.860
 And the other one is that you think a lot about food.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.500
 Has this always been the case?

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:15.900
 Not always.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.720
 I mean, I'm an older person now.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:19.940
 So I didn't grow up on a farm.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:25.540
 I grew up in Pennsylvania, a very suburban existence.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:35.350
 But suburbia then, you know, was being carved out of the forests and the old farms there in south-central Pennsylvania.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:35.530
 Yeah.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:44.930
 My grandparents were great cooks, and they had, you know, like, Concord grapes and really great apples in the backyard, and they were canners.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:50.460
 And my father was this very, very serious recreational cook.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:59.780
 And so eating at the table, it was something that you never wanted to miss at my house, because my dad was so serious about it.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.000
 And, you know, we were a pretty big extended family.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:09.220
 And, you know, holidays like Thanksgiving were just the best.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:22.630
 And as I grew up and, you know, I started to create a life for my family, a lot of my interests were really just about food and how things are made and how to do things for yourself.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.970
 And I was always interested in gardening.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:32.010
 And then, by extension, it just got to be a bigger and bigger scale.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.840
 And then, as a writer, I started to write about agriculture.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:41.050
 And I really felt to be true to my subject.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:45.170
 I had to know more, not just visit farms, but have my own.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:05:07.590
 And I think that point about seeing the land through the eyes of the farmer, that probably comes from Leopold, who was a really big influence on my thinking about owning land and taking care of land and all the problems and responsibilities and potentialities that come from agriculture.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.200
 When did you start farming yourself then?

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:10.560
 When did you?

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:12.540
 Or when did it start as a garden?

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:13.440
 How did that start?

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:13.660
 Oh, yeah.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:14.060
 Yeah.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.460
 No, I'm more like a wild garden farmer.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:23.270
 Like, I grow just enough to get myself in serious trouble and not enough to make a living.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:31.760
 And it's very much things that are easy to do, so has to fit the land.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.100
 I'm not forcing it to do much.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:45.660
 And, you know, it's just something that you learn about, and the more you get into it, the more you have to practice it.

00:05:46.450 --> 00:06:07.710
 It's a very slow endeavor, so walking and learning from other people and what they're doing and, you know, the things that they've done, I mean, it's kind of, it's not just interesting to me.

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:09.250
 It's informative to me.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:13.970
 And it's great, you know, especially up here in Northern California.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:16.660
 Well, let's talk about, let's talk about writing.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:21.580
 So you were a writer before you kind of sunk your teeth into farming, right?

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:25.340
 How did farming issues start being your subject?

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:27.580
 I think it was more ecology.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.580
 It was very organic.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.920
 You know, I really wanted to be a writer.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:42.280
 I think I really wanted to be a writer because my father was a voracious reader, and I wanted to get to him somehow.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:50.560
 And I was always pretty good, and it felt like sometimes my writing stood out.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:51.980
 But I went to school.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:57.180
 My father said, you know, you don't learn, you don't go to school to learn writing.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:00.300
 You have a very interesting life, and then you write about it.

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:14.160
 I ended up doing a lot of studying of stuff that I didn't really follow through on, and then ended up trying to teach myself how to write a little bit later than perhaps most people do.

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:24.400
 And I just flowed to where, you know, where my juice was, which was really, it was humans and the environment.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:38.670
 And at one point, I had a job, and it was with a clothing company as a writer, as a copywriter, and kind of as an eco-philosopher within this huge clothing company.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:48.150
 And we started to take apart clothing, all the fabrics and the washes and the snaps and the zippers and the everything.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:56.560
 And part of it was at the time here in California, they were growing over a million acres of cotton in California.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:04.090
 So that's been completely overtaken by almonds or ammons, as they say, out in the Central Valley.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:18.330
 I think a lot because of pesticide pressures and pest pressures and the cost and, you know, international competition, but also the cost of water growing cotton here in California in the desert.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:31.820
 And, boy, that was just an eye-opener because I got to travel all the back roads of California with this insanely great farmer named Will Allen, who he was running something called the Sustainable Cotton Project.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:35.740
 He has, since many decades ago, moved back to Vermont.

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:38.420
 He's one of the more interesting farmers you'll ever meet.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:46.280
 He's not the Will Allen who won the MacArthur Prize and does the inner-city gardening in Detroit or Milwaukee.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.280
 And, yeah, no, it's a different Will Allen.

00:08:49.900 --> 00:09:00.430
 Anyway, we one time hired this retired crop duster, and he took us up in his plane, and we had cameras, and we just followed the crop dusters.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:15.580
 And the amazing amount of spraying that what they were doing for cotton was just staggering, and it was so close to schools and irrigation ditches and water sources and communities, and it was just horrifying.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:25.320
 And so, you know, some of my early writing about agriculture was really kind of what was wrong with big industrial agriculture.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:35.730
 And then also the opposite is so, quote-unquote, alternative, you know, how it should be, how to make it, you know, as good as it can be.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:43.010
 And so that's kind of been the dichotomy of me as a writer in agriculture.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:54.380
 I'm either railing against something that's really nasty and seemingly inevitable, and then really drawn toward when it's done right.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:10:03.340
 And I get back to that when I think about, and I'm walking someone's land, and I get back to the Leopold quote.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:16.690
 You know, I think it's something he said where a thing is right when it looks right, and it's wrong when it looks wrong, and it's wrong when it goes against the biotic community.

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:20.670
 So anyway, that's a long answer.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.250
 No, that's a great answer.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:34.060
 And to me, it feels like California is one of those places in the world where there's such extremes of those models of agriculture, and a great place to think about that dichotomy.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:42.720
 I think I read your book, Farming with the Wilds, in school at UC Santa Cruz, and it must have been the year that it came out, in 2003.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:43.280
 Yeah.

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:50.860
 And that divide, it's not just in you as a writer, it's something we do with land here.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:55.120
 It's something we pull it apart, whether this part's for nature and this part's not for nature.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:56.380
 True.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:58.320
 So how did it get that way?

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.470
 Well, okay, get to California.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:15.180
 I've been thinking a lot over many years now, because I've been pulled into policy over the years, which is a really thorny, gnarly subject.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:26.950
 But I mean, I would just like to unzip California, Oregon, and Washington, and British Columbia, straight up to Alaska, and just start a new country altogether.

00:11:28.460 --> 00:11:37.980
 Because we have such similar geography, we have such similar agriculture, we have such similar public policy.

00:11:37.980 --> 00:12:02.590
 I mean, you've got really, really high value added crops on the coast, these very, very wealthy cities that are pretty liberal leaning, at least people who care to invest in the environment, in nutrition, in things that are very forward looking, maybe not enough.

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:08.370
 But I see that actually, really, when you look at Washington and Oregon and California, they're very, very similar.

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:20.570
 Farming with the wild was a subject that came to me because in the 1990s, I was able to sit in these meetings that were organized by my father-in-law.

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:23.210
 They were extraordinary meetings, a couple of them.

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:27.670
 And I was a fly on the wall, just able to participate and listen.

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:29.850
 You know, I was more of a listener.

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:48.430
 And then one meeting was some of the great conservation biologists, you know, we're talking Reed Noss and Dave Foreman and Michael Soule and all the really great minds that were telling us that our wild areas were becoming extremely isolated.

00:12:49.090 --> 00:13:05.030
 They were very interested in this whole, you know, North America, wild lands hookup and creating connections between these isolated wild lands areas in order to keep species and ecosystem processes going.

00:13:05.590 --> 00:13:10.130
 And all those connected lands, though, were really agriculture.

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:18.860
 And I think that a lot of the conservation community had a pretty negative view of agriculture for good reason.

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:36.670
 But then, you know, he organized another meeting where we had Wes Jackson from the Land Institute and our great agrarian poet, Wendell Berry, and many, many of the people that were working for different kind of agriculture.

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:41.860
 And it was amazing because they didn't really talk to each other.

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:51.910
 And it seemed like they were very, very kindred minds from the ecological viewpoint because none of them would disagree with Leopold.

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:54.450
 You know, they were all on the same page that way.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:14:02.420
 And so the idea with Farming with the Wild was to ask a couple questions.

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:07.120
 And one of the questions was, how wild can a farm be?

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:13.600
 How much biodiversity can it support and still meet the business model of the person who owns it?

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:22.770
 And alternatively, how much agriculture can take place in a wild linkage, which is really kind of a watershed.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:27.330
 It doesn't matter if you have, you know, an isolated organic farm here and there.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:30.330
 It's not going to do very much to protect wild biodiversity.

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:35.410
 You really have to have connectivity and fluidity across a landscape.

00:14:36.470 --> 00:14:45.650
 And so how much agriculture can take place in one of these vital linkages and still, you know, satisfy the biologists.

00:14:46.430 --> 00:14:53.860
 So that was about three years, really, of going around the country, in some cases around the world.

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:59.270
 But the book could have been written right here in California, you know.

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:01.670
 And it kind of started right here in California.

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:03.750
 My research started here.

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:12.650
 And I remember very clearly being up in Yolo County, up outside of Winters with this amazing man, John Anderson.

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:24.540
 And John Anderson had been a veterinarian, and he was a research veterinarian at UC Davis, which is, I think, the number one vet school in the country now.

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:28.710
 And John, though, was living in farmland.

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:30.150
 He was living on a farm.

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:37.310
 He was taught farming by his neighbors, which was this really kind of radical industrial clean farming.

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:49.890
 And for them, for those farmers up there that, you know, went all the way up into the olive-producing areas and the rice-producing areas of the Sacramento Valley,

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:54.530
 it meant vegetation-free except for the crops that you're growing.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:16:03.430
 And John, you know, he was on safaris and doing conservation work in Africa, and he saw a different kind of agriculture, and he came back.

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:08.650
 He was, you know, he thought, well, clean farming doesn't have to be vegetation-free.

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:09.970
 It could be weed-free.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:20.110
 But it doesn't mean that we can't have all of our wild edges throughout the entire state that have some kind of biodiversity value.

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:24.370
 And it doesn't mean that you're going to make your crops less productive.

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:31.870
 And so he started this heroic effort, really, of bringing all the edges of his farm back to life.

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:42.040
 And I remember standing on one side of the road, and there were John Anderson's irrigation ditches that completely had grasses and forbs and sedges.

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:46.020
 And, you know, they were starting to have some structure on them.

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:49.260
 They supported ducks and all kinds of insects.

00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:56.340
 They filtered the dirt that was coming off the roads or any kinds of chemicals that might go back into the canal.

00:16:56.560 --> 00:17:06.800
 He was so, so careful about how water came on and off of his property, and it was basically managed by putting vegetation back on the land.

00:17:07.580 --> 00:17:12.770
 And you would look across the road, and there was the exact opposite, right?

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:21.470
 Just completely bare irrigation canal, crops, nothing else but maybe, you know, canning tomatoes or something like that.

00:17:21.590 --> 00:17:29.440
 And you consider that there's 20,000 miles of irrigation canals running through California.

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:34.430
 And we could really have a completely different kind of agriculture.

00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:36.370
 It's not without effort.

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:42.800
 But when you look at all the amazing values that come of it, it is the future.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:44.920
 I mean, I don't know if we'll ever get there.

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:49.220
 It's the future in my mind of what healthy farming looks like.

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:51.040
 It doesn't look like a moonscape.

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:57.380
 It doesn't look like, you know, dirt and just super crops.

00:17:57.380 --> 00:18:07.990
 Even when they're nice and orderly, if I don't see some kind of structured wildness and habitat in and around a farm, it doesn't look healthy to me.

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:24.050
 You know, I mean, a nice dairy with cows that get hosed down and they just move from the mile-long barn into the dairy carousel to be milked and never get out on the grass.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:28.210
 That's just not healthy, no matter how it might look to your eye.

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:33.500
 Yeah, I'm just curious what you'd think, like, how we got to that place.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:35.880
 Because clean farming, you know, has a nice ring to it.

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:40.310
 They don't mean clean in terms of a lack of poisons.

00:18:40.510 --> 00:18:44.800
 They mean clean in terms of straight lines and it's easy to manage.

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.540
 You get there by cultural amnesia.

00:18:48.650 --> 00:18:55.740
 You wipe out what was there, geographical landscape amnesia, and then it's gone.

00:18:55.840 --> 00:19:00.520
 And now it's just a template for whatever you want to put down, you know.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:05.670
 I mean, we got there so slowly and so all of a sudden.

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:24.190
 But, you know, by the time Leopold was working in Wisconsin in the Dust Bowl, you know, when the soil was basically completely blowing away, they'd have decades, they'd had decades of bad farming practices trying every single crop that they could.

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:34.050
 The real tragedy of agriculture in America is, it's the tragedy of overproduction.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:37.880
 So the farmer is constantly eating his tail.

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:47.650
 The only way he can get ahead is to plant more and to hope that somebody else fails so the price goes down and he makes out.

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:51.070
 And he's only going to make out, you know, four out of ten years.

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:54.640
 So it's just this over and over thing.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:20:05.900
 And, you know, there was a certain point in time when we said that we no longer valued farm family oriented communities.

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:17.810
 What we wanted were big ass farmers that would make huge amounts of money that we could, you know, begin to export things because we weren't making things anymore.

00:20:17.810 --> 00:20:19.490
 And we needed to balance the payments.

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:29.680
 And so, you know, you ended up losing millions and millions of farmers in almost every single sector from the 40s until the 70s.

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:45.530
 Then you had in the 60s and the 70s, you had about a million people go back to the land in communes and organic farming and all kinds of small scale operations of which, you know, probably 90% of them got weeded out.

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:59.630
 Because it's so freaking hard and labor intensive and gets you into this other big dilemma, which is farm labor and the amount of, you know, just backbreaking work that goes into doing it right.

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:05.510
 And that's, you know, then you're just in the human condition cauldron.

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:09.600
 There's so many of us, you know, we're so capable.

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:17.080
 Our tools are unbelievably powerful, especially when we just focus them on the land.

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:28.300
 And our sense of restraint or our sense of, like, we don't really have a collective vision of how farming should be, unfortunately.

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:31.560
 At least we don't have the right vision, in my opinion.

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:40.400
 Not the one that says, well, you know, the farm family should be taken care of, but the water coming off the farm, you should be able to drink it.

00:21:41.150 --> 00:21:50.710
 The air and the odors coming off the farm shouldn't, you know, give people asthma and neurological injuries and all kinds of health problems.

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:56.470
 And the food that comes off the farm should really be pretty regionally oriented.

00:21:56.830 --> 00:21:58.510
 It doesn't mean it can't be experimental.

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:03.510
 But in terms of, you know, hey, let's take this idea from France, right?

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:06.350
 Salad lettuce, like, let's grow it here.

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:07.290
 Great, you know.

00:22:07.670 --> 00:22:17.430
 But we don't have to turn the Salinas Valley into this place where 70% of all the salad lettuce gets produced for the entire country.

00:22:17.810 --> 00:22:28.100
 You know, it's so, it's an industrial, capitalist, militaristic mind put on top of the landscape to feed ourselves.

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:34.240
 And it's something that, you know, if the big powers have their way, it'll be like that everywhere.

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:40.740
 Every country around the world will have a USDA, like, set up instead of their own set up.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:45.940
 Where we've worked a lot in southern Mexico, it's definitely, people are amazing farmers there.

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:52.610
 Some of the farms are crazy diverse, 60, 70, 80 different species in one, in a couple of hectares.

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:59.450
 And, but there's also a growing sector of kind of technified farming and that's pushing a monoculture.

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:01.350
 So, yeah, that's, I mean, I think that's definitely true.

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:08.470
 One of the things I remember about farming for the wild is that case you're making for a different model of agriculture.

00:23:08.650 --> 00:23:12.460
 But the other thing is like a call to collaborate.

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:23.070
 If I remember this right, it was like conservationists, farmers, food activists, we have to get together and collaborate on this different vision of agriculture.

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:27.730
 And thinking about what's happened since then, it's been a while, right?

00:23:27.730 --> 00:23:35.380
 Since 2003, there's some really disturbing things that have happened in the world of agriculture and some totally amazing things that have happened in the food movement.

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:38.330
 Yeah, I couldn't agree more than that.

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:40.970
 I mean, you have to collaborate.

00:23:41.450 --> 00:23:48.710
 Beauty is one of the things I think that perhaps a lot of people can get their minds around, you know.

00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:52.030
 And you can see when it's not right.

00:23:52.390 --> 00:24:01.710
 But sometimes our, you know, we're busy and we're focused and life is complicated and we're very urban.

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:03.490
 We're very urban people.

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:17.780
 So, you know, I think that some of the big changes that are happening right now are, let's take this situation in Des Moines, Iowa, where the water, whatever, I don't know exactly the agency,

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:30.760
 but the city of Des Moines is suing six counties that are upriver of their water source because they're farming, they're calling their farming point sources of pollution because they can't get the nitrates out.

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:40.040
 They spend millions and billions of dollars trying to clean their water and they just can't do it anymore because, well, Iowa is like the most polluted state in the country.

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:55.360
 Because wherever you have huge concentrated industrial ag and what they have there is corn, corn, corn, soy, lots of hogs, you know, huge chicken confinement operations, feedlots.

00:24:55.440 --> 00:25:07.670
 It's just like the full-on tsunami of industrial agriculture pouring through tile drains into their water systems and they can't do anything anymore, right?

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:15.110
 And so you would think if anything's going to like rally the troops around it, it's that we can't drink our water anymore.

00:25:15.460 --> 00:25:17.930
 Well, I got to do this differently, you know?

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:22.660
 Now, industrial ag is going to fight like crazy and probably win.

00:25:22.800 --> 00:25:36.190
 It's absurd because it's only, it's just prolonging the inevitable where, you know, it's unlivable and uninhabitable to be there in Iowa because it's a sacrifice zone.

00:25:37.610 --> 00:25:44.070
 You know, what a horrible way to be and not a way to work together towards something different.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:26:00.040
 And what you have here in California is a lot of people for a long time, you know, there was that conservation versus ag guys, you know?

00:26:00.100 --> 00:26:12.290
 And now you even have the social justice, economic justice versus the sustainable ag guys and everybody's learning that they have to get on the same page and it's not easy.

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:32.200
 This minimum wage, this recent uptick in wages and overtime for workers here in California, it's going to be really hard on the small growers because, you know, it's basically going to increase their labor cost, which is one of their biggest embedded costs, 30%.

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:51.460
 And so how are they going to adjust to something that's really important and really necessary, you know, so that the people who are growing the food can somehow afford it and they, or they, or even having trouble getting clean water in their communities where they have, where they're forced to live.

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:55.860
 And so there's so many forces that are bumping up against each other.

00:26:56.280 --> 00:27:10.930
 When I think about farming with the wild, when I think about like cool collaborators, the land trusts seem to be kind of the low hanging fruit of really where we should be going.

00:27:10.930 --> 00:27:27.540
 The wild farm alliance has been working with organic certifiers and they've been working on food safety laws and they've been trying to, you know, influence the good guys who have sort of vague recommendations around regulations around biodiversity protection.

00:27:28.100 --> 00:27:31.780
 The land trusts have this pretty big view of the land.

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:45.990
 And again, when I get back to farming with the wild, it's not about a beautiful farm here that has a lot of habitat because in general, if it's just an island, it doesn't have that much habitat value.

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:55.650
 It could just become a sink where all these mesopredators like to hang out because, you know, they have easy prey coming to this particular land.

00:27:55.650 --> 00:28:09.530
 And you could see it when we were studying cotton in California, you know, we'd come down to some place like in Dos Passos and Will would pull the truck up by the side of the road and he'd go, look at this tree.

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:18.400
 There's a nest in every single branch on this tree because there's nowhere else for them to go, right?

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:23.300
 And it was crazy because it's on the flyway, you know, on the Great Pacific flyway.

00:28:23.620 --> 00:28:32.190
 And so land trusts, they have a unique vision where they're trying to zip, you know, a landscape together.

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:38.830
 So even to some of the USDA biologists who are working like on the Wetlands Reserve Program or something like that.

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:48.170
 But there was a story, we were up in Washington and there was a lady and she said that she'd wanted to do this bird survey, a songbird survey.

00:28:48.170 --> 00:28:52.150
 And they were in the Metau Valley on the eastern side of Washington.

00:28:52.730 --> 00:28:58.090
 And her land trust director said, you know, nobody's going to want to participate in this.

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:02.010
 And they sent out a little, you know, postcard saying we'd like to do this.

00:29:02.370 --> 00:29:04.210
 And like everybody signed up.

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:06.610
 They wanted to know what birds were on their property.

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:21.930
 So from that, they did this very, I don't know if it was sophisticated, but they looked at the birds down by the Metau River and then the, you know, the riparian area and then the lowlands and the uplands and then way up in the mountains.

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:35.030
 And they kind of mapped where the birds were and during different seasons and they kind of prioritized this network of habitat that would really help birds throughout the Metau.

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:38.870
 And then there was a big listing of Salmonid species.

00:29:39.170 --> 00:29:48.110
 Three or four Salmonids were listed and there was a huge influx of money from the federal government and state government to protect them.

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:54.290
 And it turned out that the same thing that you did to save the songbirds helped the fish.

00:29:55.130 --> 00:30:10.320
 And now they had this map and they had these kind of priority areas that they wanted to protect and they were able to buy through permanent easements and some outright purchases and all kinds of protection this corridor throughout the Metau.

00:30:10.320 --> 00:30:19.210
 So I thought that was a really good example of a variety of different interests coming together and saving something.

00:30:19.210 --> 00:30:25.600
 I think part of the effect of separating farming from nature so rigorously is that we stop seeing biodiversity.

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:29.760
 It takes looking for those Salmonid species to actually see them.

00:30:29.900 --> 00:30:33.280
 And it's almost like it's a skill in itself to see that stuff.

00:30:33.460 --> 00:30:38.640
 Or in the biodiversity research we did in Mexico, it took walking fields with farmers.

00:30:39.520 --> 00:30:48.640
 And when we surveyed biodiversity that way, we detected twice as many species as our colleagues did just sitting down with farmers and asking about it.

00:30:48.640 --> 00:30:54.870
 So there's a skill or there's a way to relearn of even seeing the diversity that's there.

00:30:55.280 --> 00:31:02.640
 You know, I think that a lot of farmers all over the country would tell you that they're good stewards and they really care.

00:31:02.780 --> 00:31:13.970
 And that they love their animals and they love animals and, you know, and yet, you know, the erosion is just ripping across their fields in some cases.

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:21.100
 And they look at poison as something other than what it really is.

00:31:21.830 --> 00:31:36.550
 And they are misinformed by some really, really powerful interests that they find it easy to identify with because they get insurance or, you know, all kinds of things.

00:31:36.610 --> 00:31:40.970
 Because farming is really difficult and it's become so capital intensive.

00:31:40.970 --> 00:31:45.500
 But I'm losing my patience with farmers like that, you know.

00:31:45.660 --> 00:31:52.360
 And I know, you know, there's all different kinds of farmers and there's all, you know, levels of challenges.

00:31:53.020 --> 00:32:02.130
 But I just lean toward the ones like Becky Weed, who's up in Montana and she's near the Bridger Mountains.

00:32:02.890 --> 00:32:10.490
 And, you know, right outside of her sheep farm, there's like six resident bald eagles, you know, just sunning in a tree.

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:18.050
 And the wolves are not far, you know, they're kind of on that wild way in Montana.

00:32:18.490 --> 00:32:26.990
 And, you know, her point is, if I have to shoot the wolf in order to stay in business, then I got to change my business.

00:32:28.850 --> 00:32:43.060
 And so, you know, if you have to eradicate the red-winged blackbird so that you can, you know, stay in your 10,000 acre sunflower farm, then you kind of have the wrong idea.

00:32:43.060 --> 00:32:49.190
 And so, somehow, we got to support people with the right idea.

00:32:50.030 --> 00:33:06.960
 And somehow, these huge forces, you know, if we're going to make it or if the species are going to make it, which is, you know, not looking good, then we have to turn things around, you know.

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:18.800
 And that's why I do invest a considerable amount of my effort in policy now, writing and communicating about policy and the importance of it.

00:33:19.160 --> 00:33:40.300
 I'm much more excited about it at the state level and this regional level that I talked about, California, Oregon, Washington, much, much more homogeneous and possible to work together to do things right than we can at the national level, which just seems, you know, super dysfunctional.

00:33:40.300 --> 00:33:55.780
 And even more fraught with influence by the people who are really the enemies of biodiversity and they're the enemies of really, really healthy, diverse farming communities around the country.

00:33:56.320 --> 00:34:01.770
 Well, let's talk some about the Wild Farm Alliance and Watershed Media and about what you do.

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:12.920
 I guess maybe if I could tell me if this is right, if I could kind of sum up what Watershed Media does is make publications that are really focused on an issue or in an idea.

00:34:13.260 --> 00:34:16.860
 And those are very accessible, but they also kind of go deep.

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:31.550
 Reading through some of those, the question I came up with is what kinds of messages does a food movement need and how did you get into telling those stories and why does a movement like environmentalism need help telling those stories?

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:36.070
 Or help with the public relations of those ideas?

00:34:36.250 --> 00:34:38.780
 Well, everybody needs help.

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:57.090
 As I told you a little bit earlier, I worked for a fashion company and we had this really difficult challenge of describing ecological information to people who didn't really want to have ecological information.

00:34:57.210 --> 00:35:01.410
 They just wanted to buy something that made them look sexy or good, right?

00:35:01.770 --> 00:35:06.850
 And this was way back, you know, in 1990, I think we started.

00:35:06.850 --> 00:35:19.800
 So, and I began to work with this brilliant man, Roberto Cara, who's an Italian photographer, designer, and AI is just so fun to work with.

00:35:19.880 --> 00:35:23.380
 He's so talented from the visual point of view.

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:27.370
 And so, you know, we just started to make tools.

00:35:27.970 --> 00:35:30.720
 We just started to make little things, you know.

00:35:30.820 --> 00:35:40.000
 It started out a book about paper that we sent to 10,000 graphic designers because we wanted to make it easy for them to buy the right paper.

00:35:40.160 --> 00:35:41.640
 That was back in the paper age.

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:45.580
 And then, and we worked with Rainforest Action Network on it.

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:54.480
 And Randy Hayes, who, who, he did understand that there's a big role for communication in the environmental movement.

00:35:54.480 --> 00:35:56.760
 And a lot of these people, they're really smart.

00:35:57.240 --> 00:36:02.700
 And, and, and these activists, they think that they're good communicators, but they're actually not.

00:36:03.300 --> 00:36:04.770
 They could be so much better.

00:36:05.410 --> 00:36:12.870
 You know, there's a lot of things that they're, they're overlooking that Roberto continually makes me a better communicator.

00:36:13.370 --> 00:36:16.150
 So, you know, Randy said, well, now you did the paper book.

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:20.110
 Now you have to do something on building, residential building and packaging.

00:36:20.110 --> 00:36:25.730
 And then you get all of those connections of the consumer to the forest.

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:27.450
 And he helped us raise the money for it.

00:36:27.670 --> 00:36:30.890
 And, and he's been a really, really good partner for us.

00:36:31.330 --> 00:36:34.850
 You know, we were just making tools that needed to be done.

00:36:34.850 --> 00:36:41.810
 And in that one case, it was about wood and, and the average person's connection to the forest.

00:36:42.090 --> 00:36:54.070
 When we got to farming with the wild, it was trying to connect the conservation biology vision with the sustainable agriculture vision and just, you know, put something out there.

00:36:54.070 --> 00:37:00.010
 This hybrid, just ask the question and show what was being done on all these different kinds of levels.

00:37:00.790 --> 00:37:09.010
 When I was out in Sacramento one time with Randy in 2006 with Randy Hayes of Rainforest Action Network.

00:37:09.650 --> 00:37:16.270
 He was working for either the city of San Francisco or the city of Oakland at the time as an environmental director.

00:37:16.710 --> 00:37:34.790
 And we went to this conference on the farm bill and was all day long about all these extremely technical presentations about what's wrong with our farm bill, which basically spends a hundred billion dollars on food and farming in the country.

00:37:34.970 --> 00:37:42.060
 And I just left thinking, man, that was really important, but I could hardly understand any of it.

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:45.980
 Somebody has to translate this for the average person.

00:37:46.620 --> 00:37:48.620
 So, you know, that's what food fight was.

00:37:48.820 --> 00:37:52.060
 I did two food fights, two versions of that book.

00:37:52.180 --> 00:38:07.510
 And it's really just try to make this really big, fat 700 page legislation, which is so important to, you know, what we grow, how we grow it, what we eat, who gets fed, who gets support.

00:38:07.930 --> 00:38:10.870
 I just tried to make it understandable for the average person.

00:38:10.870 --> 00:38:14.050
 And, you know, it was pretty successful.

00:38:14.470 --> 00:38:16.090
 That's all I've ever tried to do.

00:38:16.310 --> 00:38:24.580
 And when I would sit down with Roberto and we would have our annual meetings of Watershed Media, I would say, well, like, what now?

00:38:24.870 --> 00:38:26.100
 What do we do now?

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:29.900
 And his answer was always the same.

00:38:30.500 --> 00:38:33.760
 We just do one good project after another.

00:38:34.210 --> 00:38:35.930
 There was never any roadmap.

00:38:36.330 --> 00:38:37.890
 There was, we're still work together.

00:38:38.310 --> 00:38:39.990
 He's 70 years old now.

00:38:40.190 --> 00:38:44.930
 We're probably doing the more fun work than we've ever done with a lot of energy.

00:38:46.520 --> 00:38:48.520
 And it's all the same.

00:38:48.680 --> 00:38:59.280
 It's just communicating and trying to find good partners that we can make resources that they'll put out, you know, if we're not working on a book by ourselves.

00:38:59.650 --> 00:39:05.340
 I think that's the unique thing about what you do is you partner with an organization to tell those stories.

00:39:05.340 --> 00:39:11.240
 But what else does it take to tell a good story about food issues or environmental issues?

00:39:12.700 --> 00:39:18.370
 Well, jeez, you probably know you're doing it.

00:39:18.700 --> 00:39:21.000
 There's, it's such a noisy space.

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:21.380
 Yeah.

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:26.580
 So, I mean, I feel like I've retreated a lot into music and other things.

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:30.930
 And, and, and, and just growing food myself.

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:35.070
 And I mean, there's so many people telling so many stories.

00:39:35.070 --> 00:39:38.070
 It's, it's amazing that we're not farther along.

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:45.850
 So, I think, I think it might be that we're not talking directly to the people we have to start talking to.

00:39:46.110 --> 00:39:50.880
 I think we have to bring what we know to the average person.

00:39:51.340 --> 00:39:58.660
 For me, my father-in-law, my late father-in-law, who taught me a lot about communication, for him, it was all visual.

00:39:59.850 --> 00:40:04.250
 And in the early days, Roberto and I, we took all the photographs for all of our books.

00:40:04.250 --> 00:40:08.150
 And we used to travel like crazy and do video, you know.

00:40:08.230 --> 00:40:10.870
 And then, I remember we did a book called CAFO.

00:40:11.570 --> 00:40:16.060
 It's all about the industrial animal agriculture business.

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:19.840
 Really, really hard to look at book.

00:40:19.840 --> 00:40:26.120
 And I got a letter from a lady who was a Tea Party farmer in Virginia.

00:40:27.140 --> 00:40:30.650
 And she said, I love your book.

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:34.960
 I, it is, it's completely changed my life.

00:40:35.770 --> 00:40:40.590
 And I take your book to meetings of the Republican Party.

00:40:40.590 --> 00:40:43.950
 And I take them to church groups all around the state.

00:40:44.170 --> 00:40:50.710
 And I become an evangelist for the need to get away from these huge industrial animal farms.

00:40:50.710 --> 00:40:53.850
 And we're being lied to by the Farm Bureau.

00:40:54.010 --> 00:40:56.950
 And we're being lied to by the Republican Party here in Virginia.

00:40:57.170 --> 00:40:57.850
 And we're being lied.

00:40:57.930 --> 00:41:04.290
 And so, this woman, you know, we sent her, obviously, we sent her tons of books, as many as she could have.

00:41:04.840 --> 00:41:08.280
 And I can't say that she was the audience.

00:41:08.480 --> 00:41:14.010
 I mean, we weren't, we weren't saying, hey, we have to go out for, you know, people who live in red states.

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:15.890
 We have to go out to really conservative people.

00:41:16.010 --> 00:41:17.570
 We were just telling a story.

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:21.220
 And we were trying to be truthful about it.

00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:23.870
 Just seeing these, these images.

00:41:23.870 --> 00:41:34.680
 I mean, the truth is that a lot of people don't get to see how their foods really produce, despite all the effort that we, you know, that has been put out there to do it.

00:41:34.720 --> 00:41:40.620
 It seems like there's still a lot of minds that need to be changed.

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:44.070
 You know, it's something I'm constantly working on.

00:41:44.070 --> 00:41:47.760
 And I think you have to change it from a lot of levels.

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:55.340
 You have to show the problem, the direness of the industrial food path.

00:41:55.480 --> 00:42:09.440
 It just makes no sense, especially from a dietary point of view and a health point of view and a clean water point of view and a point of view of future generations being able to even have the soil to grow their food.

00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:12.350
 It just doesn't and it doesn't make any sense.

00:42:13.190 --> 00:42:24.070
 And then you have to give them a vision of how can it be done in other ways and lots of creative thinking about how we can afford to pay for it.

00:42:24.190 --> 00:42:35.570
 And that kind of comes into the policy perspective, you know, then we have to somehow, you know, take money from here and invest it here so that we have this different outcome.

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:43.000
 Stories, like get so creative, get really, really simple.

00:42:45.010 --> 00:42:49.230
 And sometimes this is not easy stuff to communicate.

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:53.170
 It's really hard to tell somebody, well, you should pay more for your food.

00:42:53.290 --> 00:42:59.810
 Your food should be more expensive because that stuff that you're buying is artificially harmful.

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:05.070
 That's a really unpopular message, but we got to figure out how to do it.

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:08.680
 I think it often feels like it's a more complicated story.

00:43:09.390 --> 00:43:12.430
 How like the burdens on us to tell a more complicated story.

00:43:12.490 --> 00:43:16.250
 I'm not I'm not sure if that's just how it is or how it's been set off.

00:43:16.250 --> 00:43:27.080
 But one story that I find myself engaging a lot was this this narrative that's repeated over and again, like, well, all this organic agriculture and and good food is nice.

00:43:27.080 --> 00:43:30.940
 But we're talking about a world with nine billion people by 2050.

00:43:30.940 --> 00:43:34.320
 And we've got a greatly increased amount of food available.

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:36.940
 And I found it's such a compelling narrative.

00:43:37.360 --> 00:43:40.390
 The only problem is it's it's bull because we produce too much food.

00:43:40.530 --> 00:43:43.970
 I guess my question is, are we telling are we trying to tell more complex stories?

00:43:44.050 --> 00:43:45.930
 Are we trying to tell more nuanced stories?

00:43:46.190 --> 00:43:53.230
 Or how do we in this movement go up against some of those really well-funded, simple and compelling narratives?

00:43:53.550 --> 00:43:55.410
 Well, we just have to get better.

00:43:56.570 --> 00:44:01.920
 We have to realize that the other guys have pros, right?

00:44:01.920 --> 00:44:10.660
 They have pros that that are spinning this whole thing about how the American farmer and the American rancher are such good guys.

00:44:11.440 --> 00:44:19.510
 And, you know, it's all just life like it was when my grandparents were growing up, which was actually not that great.

00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:22.470
 But at least it wasn't as environmentally destructive.

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:27.090
 And the sophistication of it is really there.

00:44:27.310 --> 00:44:33.040
 What, you know, what I learned one day, we came across this coloring book.

00:44:33.950 --> 00:44:35.470
 And it, I know it wasn't that.

00:44:35.670 --> 00:44:36.390
 It was put out.

00:44:36.510 --> 00:44:45.140
 This guy was complaining about how hard it was for him to get his message out in Indiana as an Indiana small hog producer.

00:44:45.740 --> 00:44:53.400
 Why his small livestock operation, you know, which he did everything as best he could in a right way.

00:44:53.460 --> 00:44:58.920
 It was so hard for him to get the message about the extra value of his pork.

00:44:59.380 --> 00:45:02.350
 And so he said, and I'm up against stuff like this.

00:45:02.350 --> 00:45:10.670
 And it was a link to a coloring book that the hog producers put out with pork checkoff money.

00:45:10.670 --> 00:45:21.790
 So it's like farm bill money that's produced by a tax on every single cent of pound of pork that gets sold in the U.S.

00:45:22.210 --> 00:45:32.470
 And it was this amazing fabrication of Jimmy goes to the farm and the first person he sees, he goes to the hog CAFO.

00:45:33.070 --> 00:45:35.570
 And the first person he sees is a veterinarian.

00:45:35.830 --> 00:45:37.610
 And there aren't any veterinarians there.

00:45:37.830 --> 00:45:46.290
 Like, check out the U.S. veterinarian site and you'll see that there's a complete, there's like one veterinarian for every, you know, million hogs.

00:45:46.330 --> 00:45:47.030
 It's insane.

00:45:47.430 --> 00:45:51.760
 So the first person he meets is a veterinarian who, you know, is Jane, you know.

00:45:51.900 --> 00:45:53.400
 And then he meets the farmer.

00:45:54.050 --> 00:45:57.790
 And then the, you know, the pigs, they don't want to go outside.

00:45:57.790 --> 00:46:03.830
 They get to stay inside and crammed in these, you know, sparkly houses all day.

00:46:03.930 --> 00:46:08.790
 And then their poop is miraculously good fertilizer for the crops.

00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:18.710
 And so, and, and the bait, and then, you know, the, the pigs just become bacon, you know, with some magic, boom, you know, abracadabra.

00:46:18.810 --> 00:46:19.450
 Now they're bacon.

00:46:19.990 --> 00:46:27.500
 And, um, we put a link on our website and on our Facebook page.

00:46:27.560 --> 00:46:30.540
 And we just said, you got to check this out.

00:46:30.680 --> 00:46:32.800
 We didn't even say anything more than that.

00:46:32.840 --> 00:46:34.760
 You really have to check this out.

00:46:34.760 --> 00:46:44.760
 And to tell you the truth, it was, we got like 25,000 views, an unbelievable number of shares.

00:46:44.960 --> 00:46:51.100
 Like normally if we have 3,000 people view something that we do, we're pretty small shop, we're happy.

00:46:51.540 --> 00:46:56.120
 This was like, bang, it was, it was, it was like dynamite, right?

00:46:56.180 --> 00:46:59.180
 It was, it was one of those things that you're looking for.

00:46:59.240 --> 00:47:07.780
 And what it said to me was maybe their language and their communication is our best defense.

00:47:08.000 --> 00:47:09.870
 That's our best offense.

00:47:10.080 --> 00:47:13.410
 Let's just put their stuff out there.

00:47:13.410 --> 00:47:23.640
 So, I mean, a lot of the work that I'm doing today really is I'm, I'm taking their language with my pictures and trying to give people a reality check.

00:47:23.780 --> 00:47:34.400
 So, that's really the next frontier that we're going to, and we're just trying to make things so that you're looking there and you only have, you know, a couple seconds to grab them.

00:47:34.400 --> 00:47:44.400
 Just break it down into something simple and then bang, hopefully the light bulb's going to go off where, you know, that's gross.

00:47:44.860 --> 00:47:46.280
 But this is what they call it.

00:47:46.360 --> 00:47:49.240
 This is, this is what they're trying to tell you that it is.

00:47:49.280 --> 00:48:05.250
 And then, you know, in many states, they're trying to pass laws where it's illegal to take a photograph or a video or that the farmers have some special protection that industrial polluters or animal abusers don't.

00:48:05.250 --> 00:48:07.600
 So, like, they're untouchable.

00:48:07.720 --> 00:48:19.260
 They're trying to create this untouchable, ununderstandable, invisible industry that, you know, by many respects is really about the most destructive that we have.

00:48:19.300 --> 00:48:22.180
 And then you get back to the myth of the nine billion people.

00:48:22.440 --> 00:48:24.080
 We just have to feed them all.

00:48:24.760 --> 00:48:27.680
 You know, we're just not asking that question either.

00:48:27.900 --> 00:48:32.720
 It's like, why do we think that's a good idea, even if we could feed them all?

00:48:32.800 --> 00:48:33.840
 How are we going to feed them?

00:48:33.840 --> 00:48:40.450
 Are we going to feed them the U.S. diet that we have right now, which is just completely unhealthy and unsustainable?

00:48:41.740 --> 00:48:49.940
 You know, I mean, I don't see how the World Bank can give loans, agricultural loans,

00:48:50.250 --> 00:49:00.530
 they're not tied to also some spending to help the mothers and the girls in those communities have more education,

00:49:00.530 --> 00:49:11.050
 have a better, better chance to live than just have a whole bunch of kids and keep, you know, scratching out this agriculture that isn't working.

00:49:12.140 --> 00:49:22.310
 So, I mean, you know, it's one of those things where I think that it's greatly exaggerated that for very long,

00:49:22.370 --> 00:49:26.250
 we're going to be able to feed nine billion people the way we're feeding them today.

00:49:26.690 --> 00:49:27.090
 Yeah.

00:49:27.600 --> 00:49:28.670
 It doesn't compute.

00:49:28.790 --> 00:49:31.130
 And I got two kids, so I'm kind of worried about it.

00:49:31.310 --> 00:49:34.040
 I have a very young kid, and I'm kind of worried about it, too.

00:49:35.050 --> 00:49:39.510
 I think we could keep talking for hours, but I just wanted to ask before we ended up,

00:49:39.810 --> 00:49:46.310
 what's going on on your farm, and what are you curious and interested in experimenting with now?

00:49:46.970 --> 00:49:51.770
 I'm starting to really look at my land like I live in Santa Barbara.

00:49:53.060 --> 00:49:59.540
 So, I don't look at it differently than, so I'm planning avocados, I'm planning agaves,

00:49:59.880 --> 00:50:05.120
 I'm planning all kinds of plants that I wouldn't have thought that I could plant.

00:50:06.180 --> 00:50:11.980
 We should say for a global audience that we're, that's what, 600 miles to the south?

00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:13.700
 Yeah, that's pretty far south.

00:50:14.040 --> 00:50:19.590
 You know, that's a whole zone, at least, or a zone and a half of climate zone.

00:50:20.370 --> 00:50:24.750
 Nobody's growing avocados here, and everybody tells me I can't, but I think that I can now.

00:50:25.070 --> 00:50:29.990
 So, I just have them in very sunny places, even some right here in the backyard.

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:38.730
 And, you know, I make a lot of, I grow apples, olives, wine grapes.

00:50:39.130 --> 00:50:42.960
 I grow a lot of the vegetables that we eat.

00:50:43.060 --> 00:50:46.100
 I always have some chickens for eggs.

00:50:46.380 --> 00:50:53.250
 And so, yeah, I really love this whole eating fresh out of the garden, bottling sunlight.

00:50:53.570 --> 00:51:00.250
 So, every year I make sure I have a couple barrels of wine that I've made and, you know, 20 gallons of olive oil.

00:51:00.990 --> 00:51:24.560
 And I'm very much interested, because I've been doing it for a while now, in just a community of people who are interested in the participation of the producing and the bottling and the, you know, consumption of this, you know.

00:51:24.560 --> 00:51:27.920
 And it's for sharing.

00:51:28.120 --> 00:51:29.560
 It's like the sharing economy.

00:51:30.060 --> 00:51:41.410
 And how do you get out, you know, to this net of people who the quality is in the doing as well as in the product?

00:51:41.670 --> 00:51:52.110
 I remember being in Spain, and I was in this new olive oil facility that had been put in place by the EU and the Spanish government.

00:51:52.110 --> 00:51:59.910
 And there was this guy and this old guy, you know, it was in Andalusia where they have thousand-year-old olive trees.

00:52:00.640 --> 00:52:05.480
 And this old guy, he had all his containers around, and he had this little cap.

00:52:06.160 --> 00:52:14.830
 And I said, what are the best, what are your favorite olive varieties here, you know, in this region?

00:52:14.930 --> 00:52:18.670
 And he said, my olives are the best olives.

00:52:19.890 --> 00:52:24.550
 And so, I've really tried to take that spirit to heart.

00:52:24.550 --> 00:52:31.560
 I mean, I wake up, the first thing I care about is the plants and the animals, you know.

00:52:31.640 --> 00:52:36.460
 And I do my best, and I definitely want to give them all I got.

00:52:37.870 --> 00:52:42.610
 But after that, I have pride in the doing of it.

00:52:42.730 --> 00:52:47.110
 And I don't get too crazy about the best this or the best that.

00:52:47.270 --> 00:52:49.630
 I just try to hone my technique.

00:52:50.820 --> 00:52:51.860
 Hey, thanks so much.

00:52:51.900 --> 00:52:57.980
 I just wanted to ask where people can follow along with some of the things that you do and learn a little bit more about your projects.

00:52:58.350 --> 00:52:59.950
 Well, I appreciate your time.

00:53:00.050 --> 00:53:00.490
 I do.

00:53:00.490 --> 00:53:06.390
 And you can find us at www.watershedmedia.org.

00:53:06.970 --> 00:53:14.450
 And you can also look up what's happening at the Wild Farm Alliance at www.wildfarmalliance.org.

00:53:15.370 --> 00:53:15.660
 Easy.

00:53:15.860 --> 00:53:16.760
 Well, thanks so much.

00:53:16.840 --> 00:53:19.480
 It was really a pleasure to sit down and talk with you.

00:53:19.640 --> 00:53:20.820
 It felt like it went fast.

00:53:21.200 --> 00:53:21.600
 Thanks.

00:53:21.960 --> 00:53:22.740
 I appreciate the time.

